How to read Figure 2.5:
at \(a\) (left end) the curve is lowest of all: absolute minimum, and also a local minimum;
at \(c\) it is higher than all nearby points: local maximum (a hill top), but not the highest overall;
at \(e\) it is lower than all nearby points: local minimum (a valley);
at \(d\) it is the highest point of all: absolute maximum, also a local maximum;
at \(b\) (right end) the curve has come down to it, so it is lower than nearby points: local minimum.
At the inside points \(c\) and \(e\) the tangent is horizontal (stationary points).
